    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d274">

                <pb facs="0344" n="330" sameAs="frus:frus1944v06/pg330"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.24/1696: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Gauss</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Chungking</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1944-02-11T12:00:00+07:00">February 11,
                            1944—noon</date>.<lb/>[Received February 11—7:40 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>277. I learn from General Hearn, Chief of Staff,<anchor n="53a" xml:id="d274-f1"
                    /> that General Yu Ta-wei, Chief of Ordnance, Chinese Army, has been discussing
                    with him possible use of United States lend-lease equipment by Chinese
                    Government forces against Chinese Communists. Hearn says he informed Yu that it
                    would be impossible for Chinese successfully to explain to American people such
                    use of American equipment and later after communicating with Stilwell he
                    informed Yu that Stilwell does not wish any of the American equipped forces used
                    in Northwest. In reply to my question, Hearn said War Department had not been
                    informed.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Gauss</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d274-f1" n="53a" xml:id="d274-f1-content">
                <p>To General Stilwell.</p>
